\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Mother explains; daughter decides.\cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 They think together. Pin Cushion repeatedly gets ahead of Janine.\cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Dialogue should alter the thinking of both participants.\cell \lastrow\row
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The largest contrast is therefore not between individual facts. It is between 
\f1\b my tendency to make the causal architecture visible
\f2\b0  and your tendency to let the causal architecture support the scene invisibly.\
The spec is full of things worth knowing. The draft is full of things worth saying.\
Those are different sets.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::1>
\f1\b\fs48 Voice template\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::1><$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 Narrator\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f2 Close enough to a character to inherit their vocabulary and prejudices, but free to pull away for a dry observation.\
Capable of technical precision without adopting a solemn technical register.\
Does not police characters for truthfulness.\
Does not explain irony already produced by circumstance.\
Does not routinely tell the reader what an action means.\
A sentence can carry observation and joke simultaneously:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93She was going nowhere while it clocked distance that was all effort and no scenery.\'94\strokec2 \
The joke doesn't interrupt the physical predicament. It 
\f3\i \strokec2 is
\f2\i0 \strokec2  the description.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 Characterisation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f2 Prefer 
\f1\b behaviour that creates a character claim
\f2\b0  over a narrator supplying one.\
Pin Cushion doesn't need to be described as medically experienced. She says:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93So the sarcoglycanopathy is still there.\'94\strokec2 \
Then later:\
\strokec2 \'93Ouchies?\'94\strokec2 \
That range is the character.\
Likewise Janine's unreliability should usually emerge through what she permits herself to say, omit or believe\'97not through narration stamping 
\f1\b JANINE IS LYING
\f2\b0  on the page.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 Humour\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f2 Do not add jokes to lighten a serious scene.\
Let characters use humour because that is how they operate under pressure.\
The 
\f3\i \strokec2 perverse purse
\f2\i0 \strokec2  exchange works because it momentarily takes over the conversation. It is gratuitous in plot terms and useful in every other respect.\
Humour can therefore:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 derail exposition;\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 reveal intimacy;\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 disguise avoidance;\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 let a character recover;\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 plant later material accidentally.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 It need not justify itself immediately.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 Exposition\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f2 Default assumption: 
\f1\b cut it
\f2\b0 .\
Then restore only what the reader cannot obtain by inference.\
Preferred sequence:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 physical fact 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  character notices 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  one piece of information 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  other character infers 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  move on
\f2\b0 \
Rather than:\

\f1\b premise 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  explanation 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  qualification 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  analogy 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  risk 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  confirmation of understanding
\f2\b0 \
A useful example is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93Your muscles are different. The repair machinery is already busy. Constantly. And I can put the TPlus right beside it.\'94\strokec2 \
Pin Cushion supplies the next line of the explanation herself.\
That is close to the maximum explanatory density I should aim for when drafting new material for you.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::1>
\f1\b\fs48 Narrative-development template\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::1>
\f2 For a scene in this arc, I should begin with only four things:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 1. What has physically changed?
\f2\b0 \uc0\u8232 Something observable: bike resistance, gait, needle reaction, a result on a screen.\

\f1\b 2. Who notices first?
\f2\b0 \uc0\u8232 Often more important than the change itself.\

\f1\b 3. What does somebody want right now?
